Article 7
(1) The Regional Government is responsible for advancing women's economic independence.
(2) Women's economic independence as referred to in paragraph (1) aims to improve the independence and economic living standards of women.
(3) Women's economic independence as referred to in paragraph (1) can be implemented through:
 a. Provision of skills and job training;
 b. Ease in obtaining employment;
 c. Facilitation of the formation of productive economic business groups;
 d. Ease of access to business capital;
 e. Facilitation of marketing network development; and
 f. Ease of access to information and technology.
(4) Further provisions regarding the implementation of women's economic independence as referred to in paragraph (3) shall be regulated in a Regent's Regulation.

Article 6
(1) Women have equal opportunities without discrimination to obtain employment.
(2) Employers who employ female workers are obligated to provide protection that includes welfare, safety and health.
(3) Protection as referred to in paragraph (2) is: a. health protection/insurance;
b. health services at the workplace;
c. opportunity and facilities to express breast milk or breastfeed at the workplace at designated times;
d. obtain maternity leave for 3 (three) months after giving birth; and
e. obtain balanced nutrition for pregnant women.
(4) Employers who are unable to provide protection as referred to in paragraph (2) will be subject to administrative sanctions.
Further provisions regarding administrative sanctions as referred to in paragraph (4) are regulated in the Regent's Regulation.

